  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe key insight is that minimizing the maximum overlap integral requires identifying and aligning active and inactive regions efficiently. We use a multi-resolution strategy to escape local optima. The core idea is to:\n1. Represent the step function as a grid-based vector with specified block patterns.\n2. Evaluate solutions based on their overlap integral using direct convolution.\n3. Employ a combination of gradient-based optimization and structured local/local search guided by these patterns.\nOur primary hypothesis is that periodic patterns with controlled correlation properties exist. Unlike the original algorithm that treats the problem as continuous, we focus on piecewise constant functions identified by their active/inactive sequences. This discrete approach allows for more interpretable solutions and potentially lower correlations by design. The approach is limited by the continuous objective function but advances by embedding known underlying structures rather than relying on random search.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: Piecewise constant functions on uniform grid [0,2] with dx=2/n_points.\n- Variables: Binary vector hvals \u2208 {0,1}^{n_points} satisfying sum(h)=n_points/2.\n- Continuous embedding: For analysis, we use real-valued relaxations [0,1]^{n_points}.\n- Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k integral_{0}^{2} h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \u2248 max_k \u03b4_o(np.correlate(h,1-h,'full')) * (2/n_points)\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i and sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2 (exactly 1 with appropriate scaling - wait, note: the overall integral is 1, so sum_{i=1}^{n_points} h[i] * dx = 1, and dx=2/n_points, hence sum(h) = (n_points/2). The repair function must strictly ensure both bounds and sum constraint.\n- Smooth surrogate: E_\u03b1(h) = dx * ln( \u2211_{components} exp(\u03b1*(P_{component} - Px[idx_component])) for P derived by convolution.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Validation/Initial: \n   - For invalid h (outside [0,1] or wrong sum), use _repair function:\n        while not feasible:\n            clip values, sum discrepancy \u2192 distribute uniform adjustment across indices\n   - For parent migration, use _resample: interpolate/mean/median depending on new n_size.\n\n2. Initialization & Representation:\n   - Start with initial_h_values (if available) and construct:\n        h0 = initial_h_values.copy()\n        variants = [1-h0.copy(), 1 - (0.5+0.5*(h0-h0[::-1])), 0.5+0.5*(h0+h0[::-1]-1), ...\n   - Repair until satisfied:\n        for i in range(100):\n            clip to [0,1]\n            distribute remainder factor R to mean in directions of high gradient\n        Check sum constraint.\n\n3. Objective & Gradient:\n   - Direct evaluation: reuse np.correlate(h,1-h,'full').min() * (2/n_points)\n   - For smooth optimization (FISTA), compute gradient via discrete convolution weights and Laplace transform.\n\n4. Optimization Flow:\n   - FISTA with adaptive restarts:\n        for i in range(300000):\n            update gradient estimates if active component based on coefficient P smoother\n            projected gradient update\n            acceptance criteria based on -\u03bc C5\n   - Gradient-based search with fixed max_iter=3e7 samples\n   - Local search via pattern-based moves with active pattern selection:\n        repair function -> repair\n        four directions: P=0.2, or ask for pairing moves in opposite gradients\n\n5. Budget Handling:\n   - Use time-based budget slicing:\n        - 100s: repair/initial variants\n        - 500s: FISTA with adaptive restart at two different \u03b1 values (3 and 6)\n        - 500s: resolution migration to n=2240 then optimize using quadratic constraints\n\n6. Search Operators:\n   - Active repair: maintain sum and bounds via periodic adjustment\n   - IR Director: optimize one variable at a time? \n   - Orthogonal moves: use two-parameter gradient to maintain diversity\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- At each step:\n   - Clip, repair, and project to feasible set with tolerance 1e-12 for sum\n   - Reload best found C5 for final evaluation\n   - Check: np.correlate(best,1-best,'full').min() * (2/n) < C5_goal, then accept.\n\nConstraint handling:\n- Bounds: wrap non-feasible with _repair using tiers:\n   1. Clip values [0,1]\n   2. Distribute sum discrepancy to mean components\n   3. If still not feasible, minimize with quadratic program? (no) wait simpler settings use iterative projection\n- Sum: function variants use the provided _proj(vector, sum_desired) from the elelememntary library.\n\nResampling/rescale:\n- _resample(h, m): interpolates/extrapolates by spacing factor and then project to desired integral.\n\nSmoothed objective:\n- E_\u03b1 has global control parameter for tightness (50 to 200)\n- update payment: iterate/alpha choose during FISTA.\n\nGradient computation:\n- Use FFT convolution to estimate gradient of smooth penalized version.\n\nChoosing of candidates for local search:\n- Dynamic selection from top active components by gradient magnitude at last FISTA iteration.\n\nThis strategy overcomes local minima by introducing structure preservation and active gradient search, and shifts focus to properly defined mathematical formulation while still accepting numerical approximations for intermediate results.",
